# Normalize line endings in the repository and check them out as LF.
#
# Without this, a Windows editor (or a PowerShell `>>` / `Add-Content`
# append, which defaults to UTF-16LE in Windows PowerShell 5.1) can write
# CRLF or NUL-padded bytes into a text file. Such a file still opens fine in
# an editor but breaks markdown rendering on GitHub: the file is treated as
# binary/unrenderable and shown as plain text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts must keep CRLF even in a Linux checkout.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Real binaries: never touch line endings or diffs.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.lnk binary
